The "would you wish for it" is probably an interesting sideline here; since
all the armors are reasonably worthwhile, it's possible to wait until
you see dragons before switching over to DSM, and thus make it yourself
with just a scroll of enchant armor (and save the wish).  So it's entirely 
possible that the "wish for it" numbers, unlike vanilla, are not 
representative of what's being used.

> Green - passive poison attack --- very fun but not as good/protective
> as reflection or MR, so would you wish for it?;

Dead things don't hit you.

> Red - +d6 damage to all attacks --- meh, but I haven't tried it so...

This is probably the weakest in my opinion, but something had to be.

> Blue - The player gains the "very fast" intrinsic --- this is good,
> allows for very fast _and_ jumping boots without needing haste, but
> again not as good/protective as reflection or MR so would you wish for
> it if you didn't already have reflection and MR?;

I would wish for this in the early game every time.  The only thing I'm
risking is a random wand of death, and getting DSM AC + "very fast" while
allowing me to keep other slots free?  Oh yeah.  Especially if I haven't
seen Soko yet and might get reflection there.

> Yellow - confusion and stunning resistance --- it's wise to keep the
> scales in one's inventory but there is only one case where confusion/
> stunning is game-threatening 

I can think of at least two (Nazgul and Famine with Angel backup), but.

> and taking off one's cloak and armor to
> portal search in the planes seems rather brazen;

Three planes can be searched with cursed unconfused GD, you know.
Though the whole portal thing probably needs some work, realistically;
requiring the player to know a confused-scroll trick is probably not so
good for the "unspoiled", so to speak.

> Orange - passive sleep attack  --- very fun but not as good/protective
> as reflection or MR, so would you wish for it?;

Very little stuff is sleep resistant, and sleeping things don't hit you.
You do have to avoid waking them back up, _but_.

> Black - passive blindness attack --- cute but not as good/protective
> as reflection or MR, so would you wish for it?;

Even less stuff is blindness resistant, and while blind things _can_
hit you they have a hard time of it.

> White - variant of "water walking" --- but then how do I dip my
> potions?  I don't know if this works for the Plane of Water, but if it
> doesn't I might as well just levitate.

It does work on Water.

> One still needs MR and reflection and with a limited number of wishes,

Does one really?  I'm not so sure about that anymore.

Many of the things that one used to consider MR "mandatory" for are
toned down a bit (mostly the "ha ha" instakills), and frankly, a number
of Quest artifacts grant MR, leaving only the Rodney case.

Reflection is largely an item protection device these days; a bag does
nearly as well on that front, and you'll still need the resistances to
avoid getting plinked to death by 50% damage splash.

It would be interesting to see people try the other DSMs; honestly, I
think most people's evaluations are not based on "trying it out", but
instead on "conventional wisdom", which of course won't hold true
anymore in Spork.

